{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'historical|prior|clock|periodic|location|conditional|fit.*curve|joint' agent/journal.md | head -55; ls agent/*.py; cat agent/BUDGET.md; git status --short; tail -3 agent/tree_screen_resumed.log; tail -3 agent/stack_daily_dense.log\"","aggregated_output":"9:- Hypothesis: the small, consistent daily lattice gains and commodity/equity half-strength periodic gains survive every-prompt evaluation and their combination.\n10:- Change: evaluate `stacked` and `stacktick:gcd` for both daily competitions; additionally evaluate `stackperiodic:0.5` and `stacktick:gcd:0.5` for commodities/equities. Use 1000 paths, all development prompts, unchanged context, weights and random draws. Reuse the existing two development windows; do not inspect the final window during selection.\n17:Shallow trees improve the chronological likelihood from 1.007904 to 1.006335 with static inputs, 1.004610 with temporal summaries/harmonics, and 1.004544 after adding pretrained summaries. The preregistered selector chooses the pretrained-feature model at 200 stages. Most of the improvement clearly comes from the non-pretrained temporal/clock summaries; the marginal pretrained gain is only 0.000066. The selected model now proceeds to half/full-strength official CRPS, without field-based tree hyperparameter selection.\n26:- Change: fit Poisson-loss histogram boosting to future absolute-return scale divided by the frozen static/temporal forecast. Use seven-leaf trees, at least 150 rows per leaf, learning rate 0.03 and L2 penalty 10. Compare static-only, static plus temporal-window summaries/smooth clock harmonics, and the same features plus frozen Chronos summaries. The chronology and labels stay fixed. Select a common 0/50/100/200-stage checkpoint using only pre-field likelihood; zero stages means no correction. Bound corrections to +/-0.5 log units.\n29:- Lesson: pending. Poisson regression is used to estimate a positive conditional mean scale, not because returns are Poisson counts.\n41:The modern-stack Student alternatives fail across regimes: df4 daily CRPS ratios are COM_EQU 1.002025/0.997312 and crypto 1.000855/0.998581; df3/df8 reverse gains even more strongly. Keep df5. Daily GCD projection gives small consistent gains (COM_EQU 0.999963/0.999905; crypto 0.999888/0.999812). Half periodic correction improves COM_EQU 0.999097/0.998738 but not crypto consistently (1.000638/0.999577); continue only commodities/equities, with a joint lattice check.\n60:- Change: fit two extra seeds, 41 and 109, with exactly the current exact-context cache, 100-epoch limit, loss, regularization and chronological selector. Average log corrections with the original seed 73; test halfway and full replacement of the single temporal residual. No new labels, architecture, random path seed or clock parameters.\n65:## Iteration 34 — distribution and periodic interactions (preregistered)\n67:- Hypothesis: the stronger temporal/Sobol stack can change which finite-fan tail shape or discretization is calibrated, and a previously weak periodic residual may complement rather than duplicate the new temporal predictor.\n68:- Change: hold the stack fixed and separately test daily GCD quote projection; Sobol Student degrees 3, 4 and 8 at unchanged RMS; and the existing periodic residual at half/full strengths. Preserve draws, train cutoffs and all clock/lead settings. No new field dates or miner paths are examined.\n83:The full-temporal/Sobol stack with a 30-minute, 0.75-strength crypto clock reaches daily-crypto reward ratios 0.218222/0.126951, ranks 62/163 out of 257. A 15-minute, full-strength COM_EQU clock reaches 0.014075/0.009365, ranks 129/216. Both improve the earlier lead-only daily model in both windows at 1000 paths and all reward rounds. The half-temporal crypto alternative gives 0.249621/0.099636: a marginally higher two-window mean, but a substantially weaker minimum-window result. Prefer the common full temporal strength for robustness and fewer competition-specific strength parameters; do not claim it maximizes the two-window arithmetic mean. Promote the full stack for both daily competitions. Together with the confirmed hourly model, development Score estimates are approximately 36.724 and 33.618.\n96:- Change: preserve the exact-temporal/Sobol hourly forecast and compare two past-context grid estimators: minimum positive close difference, and a greatest-common-divisor lattice after controlled decimal conversion. Project only generated prices, never repair or round historical market data. Do not choose a new random seed or change volatility.\n103:Context-only adaptive calibration does not pass the consistency gate. Quarter-strength gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 1.00027/0.99849, hourly 1.00064/1.00012, and daily crypto 1.00002/0.99932; half strength increases regressions. Reject. The completed-volatility moment ratio is not a reliable replacement for the fixed conditional forecast.\n119:At every official reward round and 1000 paths, full exact-context temporal correction plus direct scrambled Sobol improves hourly reward ratios from 0.629991 to 0.869436 in dev1 and from 0.704092 to 0.872234 in dev2. Cumulative ranks improve 87→25 and 135→14 out of 257. Final-round ranks are 60 and 1, respectively; the latter is not the cumulative rank. Promote these hourly components with self-contained NumPy/SciPy inference in `modeling.py`. Daily remains the confirmed lead-shaped model pending its own finer-clock confirmation. Development Score estimates are now approximately 34.052 and 31.044; the reserved final window is still untouched.\n124:- Change: reconstruct nine completed static-neural volatility forecasts using only observations available at each historical anchor, compare their predicted scales with subsequently observed absolute-return scales, and shrink the ratio of means by strengths 0.25/0.5. Hourly anchors cover the past nine completed hours; daily anchors span the past three days with six-hour spacing. Skip anchors preceding weight availability and require at least three usable forecasts. Cap the unshrunk ratio to [0.5,2].\n135:- Hypothesis: the previous score-curve fit used a flat classical path family at 128 paths, while deployment now includes learned scale and clock/lead profiles. Training and inference objectives therefore differed even after correcting raw-score weighting.\n137:- Result: pending. These are in-sample/meta-training response curves around fitted parameters, not historical causal backtests; only later development-window evaluations are performance claims. No field-rank calculation uses these training dates.\n141:## Iteration 29 — finer-clock interaction and joint normalization (preregistered)\n143:- Hypothesis: finer empirical clocks may complement the temporal residual. Separately, normalizing clock and lead components individually does not guarantee that their product has unit mean; the earlier description of perfectly preserved average scale was too strong.\n144:- Change: around full exact-temporal plus half-lead, compare 30-/15-minute clocks, full clock strength at 15-minute resolution, and explicit unit-mean normalization of the final product. Each comparison has an adjacent one-variable control. All forecasts remain positive and continuous-trading, with no sessions or zero-volatility gates.\n145:- Result: a 16-context diagnostic finds joint mean multipliers from 0.9992 to 1.0252 despite unit-mean components. Existing held-out forecasts and rewards remain valid; only the previous interpretation needs correction. The simpler clock screen gives consistent equity gains at 30-/15-minute resolution (ratios 0.99761/0.99799 and 0.99659/0.99745). Crypto favors the more conservative 30-minute profile (0.99929/0.999994). Lowering the floor alone is effectively a null change in this sample.\n149:## Iteration 28 — empirical clock resolution and shrinkage (preregistered)\n151:- Hypothesis: the current hourly clock and three-bin smoothing may blur repeatable volatility bursts. Its 25% profile floor and 25% shrinkage also enforce substantial dispersion in empirically quiet periods. These are modeling choices, not venue constraints.\n159:Forecast-error mixtures fail the consistency gate at 1000 paths. Half conditional replacement gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 0.99987/1.00065, hourly 1.00007/1.00038, daily crypto 1.00015/1.00099. Full replacement regresses further, and conditioning on the recent volatility trend barely differs from pooling. Discard these mixtures. The simpler Student mixture remains better calibrated than this empirical future-volatility uncertainty proxy.\n166:- Verdict: retain only a consistent gain; otherwise keep the simpler Student mixture. The temporal and periodic mean-scale models remain separate experiments.\n169:## Iteration 26 — smooth data-fitted periodic scale (preregistered)\n171:- Hypothesis: continuously traded markets can have smooth activity cycles even without sessions or closures. Past-week clock shape does not explicitly forecast how average future volatility depends on the current phase of those cycles.\n174:- Verdict: require consistent gains; reject rather than invent a trading-session explanation if the data do not support this periodic predictor.\n179:All three ridge penalties worsen chronological validation mean-square error versus zero location: baseline 1.82452; penalties 100/1000/10000 give 1.84238/1.84066/1.83205 on 2,208 validation rows (20,215 training rows). The selector correctly exports zero coefficients. Reject before spending on redundant CRPS screens, because every proposed location path would equal the incumbent. Keep zero log drift; no evidence supports a directional adjustment.\n185:## Iteration 25 — conditional price location (preregistered)\n187:- Hypothesis: zero conditional log drift may leave small mean-reversion or momentum information unused. This is distinct from the previous experiments on volatility reversion and serial covariance.\n188:- Change: fit a heavily regularized ridge model for standardized future log returns at 5, 60 and 1440 minutes, from the original static inputs plus ten trailing signed-return horizons and their availability indicators. Select the ridge penalty only on the pre-July-26 chronological holdout, compare to a zero forecast, cap each predicted location at 0.3 reference standard deviations, and test quarter/half strengths against the promoted lead-shaped incumbent. No external prices or directional labels after the training cutoff.\n190:- Verdict: discard unless location helps both development windows and does not harm another competition materially; no directional story will substitute for measured performance.\n235:- Hypothesis: training a daily model on future one-minute absolute returns can miss the five-minute microstructure that dominates daily CRPS. Predicting future five-minute absolute returns directly may improve calibration, even though changing the historical feature lag alone did not pass the earlier consistency gate.\n236:- Change: keep the original feature rows, model architecture, optimizer and chronological split; replace only the daily target with the mean future five-minute absolute log-return divided by sqrt(5), on overlapping observed endpoint pairs within the next 24 hours. No interpolation, and all target endpoints remain before July 26. Keep the original hourly target for joint training but do not deploy its changed head in this experiment.\n243:- Hypothesis: independent Student mixing scales currently break exact antithetic symmetry. Paired scales or low-discrepancy joint draws may improve finite-ensemble CRPS without changing the intended predictive law.\n267:- Hypothesis: a flat learned daily scale cannot represent conditional volatility relaxation after a recent shock. Predicting positive volatility in five future buckets can improve the path's allocation of uncertainty without changing its average scale.\n268:- Change: train a pooled five-output network on snapshot-only future absolute-return scales in minutes 0–60, 60–180, 180–360, 360–720, and 720–1440. Reuse the original 30 features and chronological training/validation split, with all targets ending before July 26. Normalize the predicted bucket scales to unit duration-weighted mean, then blend only the shape with the incumbent daily local-clock profile.\n271:- Lesson: pending. This isolates conditional lead time rather than revisiting fixed exchange sessions or hand-coded volatility decay.\n373:## Iteration 9 — empirical periodic volatility, not sessions (preregistered)\n375:- Hypothesis: a flat daily variance forecast misses repeating information-flow patterns even on a venue that trades continuously. A weak periodic profile estimated exclusively from the supplied past seven days may improve daily paths.\n376:- Change: normalize past absolute returns within each observed calendar day, estimate a smoothed 24-hour profile, and shrink toward a flat positive curve. Test strengths 0.25/0.5/0.75/1 against the full daily-neural incumbent. This has NO exchange calendar, open/close boundary, weekend switch, or zero-volatility session. Every hour remains active; this experiment tests whether a periodic pattern exists rather than assuming one.\n378:- Verdict: retain only if both development windows show consistent official CRPS gains; otherwise discard the periodic hypothesis.\n383:The empirical positive volatility profile clears the gate. Relative to the daily-neural incumbent, strength 0.75 gives COM_EQU_24H 0.98653/0.98038 and daily crypto 0.99567/0.99463; strength 1 gives 0.98496/0.97800 and 0.99577/0.99467. Hourly is unchanged. Thus continuous trading does not imply a flat variance clock in this snapshot. Prefer the slightly shrunk 0.75 profile provisionally and validate full field emissions at 1000 paths; do not reinterpret this as exchange-session closures.\n403:Moment matching does not reveal a consistent material improvement. Student df3 at matched absolute scale 1.0 gives hourly 0.99920/0.99939 but daily-crypto 1.00044/1.00166; other scale choices trade gains in the calmer window for losses in the earlier window. Retain df5 at the existing scale. This confirms that the prior simple mixture was near a broad calibration optimum; it does not prove all heavy-tailed models are equivalent.\n405:## Iteration 11 — periodic-profile stability (preregistered)\n407:- Hypothesis: six or seven observed days make the local variance profile noisy. A strictly pre-field historical profile can regularize it without any assumed exchange calendar or zero-variance periods.\n408:- Change: fit positive per-asset hourly profiles from April 1 (or first trade) through July 26, using the same within-day normalization and circular smoothing. Blend 25/50/75/100% historical prior into the local profile; keep total cycle strength 0.75. Separately check 15/30/60/120-minute robust half-lives for hourly forecasts, changing nothing else.\n409:- Result: pending official development-window CRPS. The historical profile never reads validation-period prices when fitted.\n415:Keep local-only profile and 60-minute hourly memory. Historical profile shrinkage has <0.1% gains and mixed COM_EQU performance (100% prior 0.99930/1.00120). Hourly 15/30/120-minute half-lives versus 60-minute produce 1.00186/1.00422, 1.00008/1.00028, and 1.00135/1.00211. The additional parameters are not justified.\n508:- Change: reuse the same official CRPS response curves, network, split, and optimizer, but weight raw scores by the validator asset coefficients and balance historical asset sample counts. Normalize only by a constant training-set scale per competition for numerical conditioning. This avoids outcome-dependent per-prompt normalization. No additional field history is needed, because a common prompt-minimum subtraction cancels in complete-coverage miner comparisons.\nagent/adaptive.py\nagent/auxiliary.py\nagent/chronos_vol.py\nagent/clock_profile.py\nagent/field_eval.py\nagent/fit_chronos.py\nagent/fit_clock.py\nagent/fit_periodic.py\nagent/fit_score_curves.py\nagent/fit_shape_score.py\nagent/fit_tree.py\nagent/fit_uncertainty.py\nagent/fit_weighted_score.py\nagent/location.py\nagent/modeling.py\nagent/periodic.py\nagent/predict.py\nagent/rank_screen.py\nagent/refit_vol.py\nagent/refresh_vol.py\nagent/research.py\nagent/sampling.py\nagent/temporal.py\nagent/train_auxiliary.py\nagent/train_ensemble.py\nagent/train_fiveminute.py\nagent/train_lead.py\nagent/train_location.py\nagent/train_temporal.py\nagent/train_vol.py\nagent/tree_vol.py\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 50.41 / 100.00 USD (50%)\n- Time   : 0.0 h elapsed, 18.0 h remaining (0%)\n- Phase  : normal\n M .gitignore\n M agent/journal.md\n M agent/modeling.py\n M agent/research.py\n M agent/sampling.py\n M agent/temporal.py\n M agent/train_temporal.py\n M pyproject.toml\n M uv.lock\n?? agent/chronos_calibration_weights.json\n?? agent/chronos_calibration_weights.npz\n?? agent/chronos_vol.py\n?? agent/fit_chronos.py\n?? agent/fit_tree.py\n?? agent/results/refresh_daily_dense.parquet\n?? agent/results/refresh_daily_dense_template_step12_ranks.json\n?? agent/results/stack_interactions.parquet\n?? agent/results/starter_probe/\n?? agent/results/temporal_ensemble_screen.parquet\n?? agent/results/ticked_hourly_dense_template_step1_ranks.json\n?? agent/temporal_seed109_weights.json\n?? agent/temporal_seed109_weights.npz\n?? agent/temporal_seed41_weights.json\n?? agent/temporal_seed41_weights.npz\n?? agent/tree_vol.py\n?? agent/tree_weights.json\n?? agent/tree_weights.npz\ndev1 ETH 86400: 60 prompts, 3 variants\ndev1 ETH 3600: 60 prompts, 3 variants\ndev1 SOL 86400: 60 prompts, 3 variants\nNVDAX filled 132/132 price-gap prompts from validator realized paths\nGOOGLX filled 220/220 price-gap prompts from validator realized paths\nHYPE filled 14/14 price-gap prompts from validator realized paths\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
