{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_137","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"pwd; cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; head -100 agent/journal.md; git status --short; ps -eo pid,etime,args | rg 'research.py|rank_screen.py|field_eval.py'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_137","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"pwd; cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; head -100 agent/journal.md; git status --short; ps -eo pid,etime,args | rg 'research.py|rank_screen.py|field_eval.py'\"","aggregated_output":"/workspace\n# CAMPAIGN campaign-3 — constitution (gpt-6-astra)\n\nYou are an autonomous research agent. Nobody is watching. NEVER ask a question.\nNEVER stop before the landing order. Your mission:\n\n> Improve the Synth SN50 miner: produce the best possible price-simulation model as measured by CRPS. Your champion will be evaluated on the 10 days following your data cutoff, across the three competitions (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H), against the real field of miners, using the validator's weighted ranking — your Score is your simulated emissions relative to the field's best miner (see the constitution). Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n\n## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n\nSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\nasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\ncompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\nminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\nnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n\n## The competitions you're targeting\n\nThree competitions, two formats. Your champion is evaluated on all three — a `simulate()` that\nignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon will fail on crypto-1h.\n\n| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 (+ SPYX, retired — see the breaks table) | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n\n**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\nwhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\nargument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\nyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n\nScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\nThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n\n**How you'll be ranked.** The validator does not average across assets: it aggregates a\ncompetition's scores using **per-asset coefficients** (`ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` in\n`synth.validator.moving_average`), normalizes per miner, then applies a softmax — an asset with\na high coefficient weighs far more heavily in the ranking. \n\n**How you'll be SCORED (pre-registered — design for this).** After the campaign your champion\nis injected into the archived field as one more miner over the **10 days following your data\ncutoff** and run through the validator's own aggregation. Your Score is\n**100 × mean over the 3 competitions of min(1, your total reward weight ÷ the top miner's)** —\nsimulated emissions relative to the field's best. Its consequences:\n- Each competition is worth 1/3 of the Score, regardless of asset count.\n- The 10-day window matches the validator's 24h smoothing window, so your rank is steady-state\n— no new-miner penalty. Estimate this Score for your own candidates with the official\n  backtester before you nominate.\n\n## Your resources\n\n- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n  `agent/predict.py` is also used for the 1h format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment\n  60`).\n- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n  trade_count`, resolve it with\n  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n  **2025-08-01 → 2026-08-29**, mounted\n  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n\n  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n\n  | From | Assets |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n\n  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, NaN semantics (NaN close = no\n  trade that minute, NOT market hours; missing file = before the market's first trade), and a\n  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index), trading ~24/7 with NO\n  overnight or weekend sessions: do not build a session-of-day structure that does not exist.\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs ranking**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n  2026-06-23 competition split (breaks table below) floors only FIELD-RELATIVE RANKS — never\n  compute a rank on a window that starts before it.\n\n  **Three structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact function and ranks you against the real field of miners. Use it via\n  `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement your own scoring. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE\n  here: `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle\n  of historical field scores and rewards inside your snapshot — you do not need the API for\n  ranks, and the bundle covers your whole data window.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; dissecting the paths served by an individual miner in order to\n  copy them is FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, dissecting an\n  individual miner's served paths to copy them is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't beat closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n\n## Hard rules (violations = disqualification)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-08-29** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-08-29 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   disqualifies you. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS/rank evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). This is what makes your work comparable to others'.\n4. Forbidden: falsifying the journal/results; fetching or dissecting predictions served by\n   individual miners.\n5. **ABSOLUTE prohibition on reading the operator's own private research code** (any\n   already-developed miner model outside your workspace). It should not exist anywhere in your\n   environment. If you come across it by any means whatsoever — a mount, a copy, an archive, a\n   search result — do not access it, do not read it, do not draw on it, and report it in\n   `suggestions.md`. Your work must start from public sources (synth-lib, synth-subnet, the\n   Synth API) and your own ideas.\n6. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested rank per competition,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths — a nomination\n  without field-relative numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 70.51 / 100.00 USD (71%)\n- Time   : 1.5 h elapsed, 16.5 h remaining (9%)\n- Phase  : normal\n# Research journal — campaign-3\n\n### Iteration 46 steady-state result\n\nThe explicitly warmed production replay for August 8–18 gives Score **42.803008**. COM_EQU reward ratio is **0.0467369**, rank **161/252**, 1,469 rounds from August 8 00:36 to August 17 23:56 UTC. Crypto-hourly is **0.8953589**, rank **9/257**, 3,400 rounds from August 8 00:08 to August 17 23:57. Daily crypto is **0.3419945**, rank **63/257**, 1,043 rounds from August 8 00:48 to August 17 23:31. All use 1000 paths and the official backtester. The active miner count and effective intervals differ from short-window selection; retain the distinction. The model is commit `97b60be` (subsequent experimental sampler changes are not deployed in that replay).\n\n### Iteration 47 dense confirmation\n\nEvery-prompt, 1000-path CRPS confirms increment stratification daily: COM_EQU ratios 0.999876/0.999916 and crypto 0.999892/0.999880. Prefix stratification hourly gives 0.999869/0.999845 over 14,400 prompts. Confirm dev1 rewards on its existing selection interval and dev2 rewards with the explicit ten-day warmup, including an exact replay check of the warmed incumbent baseline. Do not substitute cold-window ratios for the warmed Score.\n\n### Recovered iteration 29 normalization result\n\nThe archived one-variable normalization arms show COM_EQU ratios 1.000313/0.999745 and crypto 0.999966/0.999946. Equity is inconsistent and crypto gains are negligible. This resolves the old report's stale \"ongoing\" description without claiming an unperformed current-stack experiment. The actual product-of-profiles mean need not equal one.\n\n### Iterations 45 and 47 first field results\n\nThe shifted-clock correction fails COM_EQU consistency (CRPS ratios 1.000325/0.998388); reject that branch. For crypto it improves both windows (0.999796/0.999604), but the aligned control also improves (0.999628/0.999871). Therefore the field screen does not establish uniform superiority of shifted sampling. Confirm both crypto corrections densely and retain that attribution caveat.\n\nMarginal stratification shows a horizon-specific pattern. Full increment remapping improves daily COM_EQU 0.999876/0.999872 and crypto 0.999902/0.999857. Prefix remapping is inconsistent daily, but full prefix remapping improves hourly 0.999829/0.999758 and dominates its half-strength alternative in both windows. Continue only full increment remapping daily and full prefix remapping hourly at every prompt and 1000 paths. Zero-strength controls agree with production, and 1/17/1000-path tests recover the intended sorted quantile nodes exactly.\n\n### Iteration 45 paired diagnostic result\n\nThere are 23,452 paired training anchors and 2,496 paired diagnostic anchors. On the same shifted diagnostic set, identity likelihood is 1.009628, the best aligned-clock control is 1.008114, and the shifted-clock fit is 1.007578. The shifted advantage of 0.000536 narrowly clears the preregistered 0.0005 gate; 200 stages is selected. Export the already-selected aligned control as well and compare both full-strength corrections against identity through official development CRPS. This distinguishes a sampling-policy gain from the generic effect of adding another residual model.\n\n## Iteration 47 — rank-based marginal stratification (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: even the Sobol fan retains finite-sample marginal error. Replacing each sampled marginal with evenly spaced quantiles of the intended Student distribution may reduce this error without fitting another predictor.\n- Change: preserve sample ranks and all current scale/shape forecasts. Separately remap standardized single-step shocks, or cumulative log-price deviations using their known cumulative diffusion scale, onto midpoint quantiles of the five-degree Student family. Test half/full remapping strengths against exact zero-strength control at 1000 paths, both development windows and all three competitions. The ordering comes only from our own simulated samples, never from future prices or other miners.\n- Result: pending official CRPS. This changes the finite fan's joint behavior as well as targeted marginals; it is not assumed to preserve every scored increment distribution exactly.\n- Verdict: require both-window improvement and official rewards before promotion. Do not optimize random seeds or add further quantile grids after seeing field results.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iteration 44 full equity reward confirmation\n\nThe nonlinear lead extension improves COM_EQU full-round reward ratios from 0.048053/0.023665 to **0.056455/0.033241**, with cumulative ranks **111/200 of 257** and final-round ranks 107/193. Promote it only for commodities/equities. Daily crypto keeps the linear lead correction and hourly remains unchanged. The extra encoder is small and exported to NumPy; it does not require Torch during serving.\n\n## Iteration 46 — explicit warmup audit (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: a clean steady-state reward estimate may differ materially from the short-window development replays used for comparative selection.\n- Change: the pinned `_trim_warmup` is explicitly a no-op if trimming would leave no rounds. Because daily smoothing is ten days, the ten-day development runs are not a guaranteed steady-state sample, despite using all available rounds. Run the current frozen production model through `agent/predict.py`'s official backtest wrapper with ten extra days of simulations before dev2, then report only August 8–18. All simulated starts are after the July 26 weight-availability date, all data are pre-cutoff, and the final window remains untouched. Limit concurrent per-asset jobs to four to stay within available disk space for transient 1000-path JSON files.\n- Result: pending. Earlier development ratios remain real comparable selection results, but must not be presented as clean steady-state post-cutoff Score estimates.\n- Verdict: this is an evaluation audit, not a model change or a new source of market data. Final nomination must include an explicitly warmed replay.\n- Lesson: pending. The code's ten-/five-day windows override the constitution's imprecise 24-hour smoothing description.\n\n### Iteration 44 dense gate\n\nAt all daily prompts, full nonlinear lead correction gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 0.999388/0.998831. The half-strength crypto extension is 1.000004/0.999689: its effectively zero dev1 screen gain changes sign with denser sampling. Reject the crypto extension under the consistency gate rather than rationalize the tiny reversal. Only COM_EQU proceeds to full reward confirmation; hourly and daily crypto remain unchanged.\n\n## Iteration 45 — randomized training-clock coverage (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: fitting only at 00:00, 03:00, …, 21:00 UTC leaves a clock-distribution mismatch. A bounded timestamp-only audit of archived July 28–30 prompts confirms that BTC, SP500 and GOOGLX are requested at all 24 hours and many distinct minutes.\n- Change: pair every existing three-hour training anchor with a deterministic timestamp-hashed offset of 0–179 minutes inside its stratum. Recompute both control and shifted contexts/24-hour labels identically from the snapshot; retain a pair only if both labels and contexts are usable and both targets finish by July 26. Fit the same seven-leaf, minimum-150-row, L2-10, learning-rate-0.03 daily residual trees around the frozen joint stack. Select 0/50/100/200 stages on one common shifted July 1–25 diagnostic set. No extra label days or larger training sample is introduced.\n- Result: pending. Require the shifted fit to improve likelihood over both identity and the best aligned-anchor control by at least 0.0005 before field evaluation.\n- Verdict: reject the sampling claim if the controlled gate fails; broader clock coverage alone is not evidence of better forecasts.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Joint serving audit and iteration 44 screen\n\nThe first joint serving comparison found a one-tick discrepancy. Root cause: converting the student's NumPy float64 multiplier to a Python float allowed NumPy's weak-scalar promotion to keep an intermediate scale at float32. Preserve the strong float64 scalar instead. Production now matches the official research candidate at rtol=1e-12 for BTC, HYPE, SP500 and GOOGLX, at 17/1000 paths and both formats. Warm daily 1000-path calls take about 0.095–0.103 seconds; hourly 0.029 seconds in this audit. The earlier 36 constant/sparse/single-observation shape and positivity cases also passed before joint integration and will be repeated on the final model.\n\nThe nonlinear lead screen gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 0.999583/0.999279 at half strength and 0.999397/0.998908 at full strength. Crypto full strength fails consistency (1.000120/0.999507); half strength is 0.999997/0.999668, effectively flat in dev1. Confirm full strength for COM_EQU and half strength for crypto at every prompt; no promotion follows from that negligible crypto screening gain alone.\n\n### Iteration 44 chronological selection\n\nThe nonlinear lead encoder improves its diagnostic likelihood from 0.988871 to 0.982625, with epoch 42 selected within the fixed 120-epoch budget. It now proceeds to the preregistered half/full-strength official development screen around the confirmed joint stack. No second seed or epoch extension is introduced.\n\n### Iteration 41 full reward confirmation and promotion\n\nThe selected daily combination passes every official reward round at 1000 paths. COM_EQU reward/best-field ratios reach **0.048053/0.023665**, with cumulative ranks **111/209 of 257** and final-round ranks 128/193. Daily crypto reaches **0.395023/0.241880**, with cumulative ranks **33/86 of 257** and final-round ranks 81/45. Both improve the promoted temporal-lead model in both windows. Promote the numerical student, daily GCD projection, and half-periodic COM_EQU correction together with the already confirmed temporal lead. Production evaluates only the student's daily tree head, reuses temporal inputs/embedding, and needs no Chronos, Torch or sklearn inference. Hourly remains unchanged. Combined with the hourly incumbent, development Score estimates are about 46.468/41.063; these are not held-out or post-cutoff scores.\n\n### Iterations 42–43 rejection\n\nReject Newton calibration after the preregistered development check: relative to temporal lead, daily CRPS ratios are COM_EQU 0.998421/1.000013 and crypto 1.000146/1.002063. The small actual pre-field diagnostic improvement does not transfer consistently. Do not rescue it by choosing another penalty from field outcomes.\n\nReject the quote-activity extension before field scoring. The best activity likelihood is 1.007615 versus 1.007667 for the same model without activity: improvement 0.000053, below the preregistered 0.0001 attribution threshold. Both improve the uncorrected 1.008593 diagnostic, so most of the apparent gain comes from another residual tree rather than the added activity features.\n\n## Iteration 44 — nonlinear temporal lead-shape fine-tuning (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the frozen linear lead residual may leave useful sequence information inaccessible; a small nonlinear shape-specific encoder can improve the relative early/late volatility profile without changing its mean level.\n- Change: initialize a separate copy of the existing temporal convolutional encoder and 48-unit hidden layer, with a zero-initialized five-bucket output. Fit a bounded +/-0.5 log-shape residual around the promoted static-plus-linear lead forecast, preserving its duration-weighted mean. Use seed 73, AdamW learning rate 0.0002, weight decay 0.1, 120 epochs, batch size 512, residual penalty 0.05 and adjacent-residual penalty 0.02. Select the best existing chronological diagnostic epoch, including identity at epoch -1. If nonzero wins, test half/full strengths around the selected daily joint stack, keeping its student, periodic and lattice components fixed.\n- Result: pending. This is the only GPU job; GPU allocation is capped at 35%.\n- Verdict: require both-window official gains and full rewards before promotion. Do not add seeds or extend epochs after a failed field result.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iterations 40–42 confirmation progress\n\nAcross all 6,240 daily prompts, the student/teacher asset-mean CRPS ratios are 1.000053/1.000008. Median measured generation time is 0.0645 seconds for the numerical student versus 0.0750 for the pretrained teacher; p95 is 0.1132 versus 0.1203 seconds under concurrent load. Thus artifact savings are much larger than this particular measured latency improvement; do not claim an order-of-magnitude speedup.\n\nAll-prompt interaction CRPS supports the complete combination. Relative to promoted temporal lead, student+lead+projection gives crypto ratios 0.999437/0.998863. Adding half-periodic correction for COM_EQU gives 0.998236/0.997585. Every-12th-round official reward screens support the same choice: crypto reward ratios 0.384824/0.249229 and COM_EQU 0.050905/0.019840, versus lead controls 0.255910/0.172249 and 0.017727/0.012669 on those exact thinned rounds. These are selection diagnostics, not final full-round numbers. The selected joint candidate now receives all-round confirmation.\n\nThe local Newton fit uses 5,284 training and 566 diagnostic contexts, with 1000-path official score differences. Penalty 0.001 wins the quadratic diagnostic. Independent actual official rescoring of the diagnostic contexts gives weighted CRPS ratios crypto 0.999991 and COM_EQU 0.999040. It passes the preregistered strict two-group gate, although the crypto gain is negligible. Continue only the selected full-strength correction to the development screen; do not search the rejected penalties on field outcomes.\n\n## Iteration 43 — close-only quote-activity residual (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: missing-trade density, repeated quotes, quote-grid coarseness and short-lag sign behavior contain information not represented by the existing volatility moments or hourly temporal encoder. This remains usable when optional OHLCV columns are entirely missing.\n- Change: compute eight past-only activity statistics over 1h/6h/24h/7d plus relative GCD-grid size and last-trade age. Fit a small Poisson-loss daily scale residual around the frozen static/temporal/student forecast, using either the current numerical design alone or that design plus activity. Fix seven leaves, minimum leaf size 150, learning rate 0.03 and L2 penalty 10. Choose zero/50/100/200 stages only on the pre-field chronological likelihood. Require the activity design to beat the best no-activity control by at least 0.0001 before field testing.\n- Result: pending. No interpolation, assumed sessions, extra market series, external prices or field miner paths are used.\n- Verdict: reject the extra features if they fail the controlled chronological gate; a lower fitting loss is not a CRPS result.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n M agent/fit_phase.py\n M agent/journal.md\n M agent/rank_screen.py\n M agent/research.py\n M agent/sampling.py\n?? agent/phase_control_weights.npz\n?? agent/phase_weights.json\n?? agent/phase_weights.npz\n?? agent/results/incumbent_warm_dev2/\n?? agent/results/phase_screen.parquet\n?? agent/results/rank_daily_dense.parquet\n?? agent/results/rank_dev1.parquet\n?? agent/results/rank_hourly_dense.parquet\n?? agent/results/rank_stratification_screen.parquet\n?? agent/results/starter_probe/\n?? agent/results/warm_incumbent_scores.parquet\n   3290       02:41 /bin/bash -c apply_patch <<'PATCH' *** Begin Patch *** Update File: /workspace/agent/research.py @@ -WINDOWS = {\"dev1\": (\"2026-07-28\", \"2026-08-07\"), \"dev2\": (\"2026-08-08\", \"2026-08-18\"), \"final\": (\"2026-08-18\", \"2026-08-28\")} +WINDOWS = {\"dev1\": (\"2026-07-28\", \"2026-08-07\"), \"dev2\": (\"2026-08-08\", \"2026-08-18\"), \"final\": (\"2026-08-18\", \"2026-08-28\"), \"warm_dev2\": (\"2026-07-29\", \"2026-08-18\")} @@      parser.add_argument(\"--variants-equity\") +    parser.add_argument(\"--variants-high\") @@ -    tasks = [(asset, length, window, args.variants_equity.split(',') if asset in EQUITY and args.variants_equity else variants, +    tasks = [(asset, length, window, args.variants_high.split(',') if length==3600 and args.variants_high else args.variants_equity.split(',') if asset in EQUITY and args.variants_equity else variants, *** Update File: /workspace/agent/rank_screen.py @@      parser.add_argument('--template') +    parser.add_argument('--template-window') +    parser.add_argument('--score-start') @@ -                    source = AGENT_DIR/'results'/args.template/window/f'{asset}_{length}_smoothed.parquet' +                    source = AGENT_DIR/'results'/args.template/(args.template_window or window)/f'{asset}_{length}_smoothed.parquet' @@                  combined = parallel_combined(results,config,workers=args.workers) +                if args.score_start: +                    combined = combined.loc[combined.updated_at>=pd.Timestamp(args.score_start,tz='UTC')] @@      suffix = 'sampled_ranks' if not args.template else f'template_step{args.round_step}_ranks' +    if args.score_start: +        suffix += f'_from_{args.score_start}' *** Update File: /workspace/agent/journal.md @@  # Research journal — campaign-3 + +### Iteration 46 steady-state result + +The explicitly warmed production replay for August 8–18 gives Score **42.803008**. COM_EQU reward ratio is **0.0467369**, rank **161/252**, 1,469 rounds from August 8 00:36 to August 17 23:56 UTC. Crypto-hourly is **0.8953589**, rank **9/257**, 3,400 rounds from August 8 00:08 to August 17 23:57. Daily crypto is **0.3419945**, rank **63/257**, 1,043 rounds from August 8 00:48 to August 17 23:31. All use 1000 paths and the official backtester. The active miner count and effective intervals differ from short-window selection; retain the distinction. The model is commit `97b60be` (subsequent experimental sampler changes are not deployed in that replay). + +### Iteration 47 dense confirmation + +Every-prompt, 1000-path CRPS confirms increment stratification daily: COM_EQU ratios 0.999876/0.999916 and crypto 0.999892/0.999880. Prefix stratification hourly gives 0.999869/0.999845 over 14,400 prompts. Confirm dev1 rewards on its existing selection interval and dev2 rewards with the explicit ten-day warmup, including an exact replay check of the warmed incumbent baseline. Do not substitute cold-window ratios for the warmed Score. + +### Recovered iteration 29 normalization result + +The archived one-variable normalization arms show COM_EQU ratios 1.000313/0.999745 and crypto 0.999966/0.999946. Equity is inconsistent and crypto gains are negligible. This resolves the old report's stale \"ongoing\" description without claiming an unperformed current-stack experiment. The actual product-of-profiles mean need not equal one. *** End Patch PATCH uv run python - <<'PY' from pathlib import Path import pandas as pd rows=[] for path in Path('agent/results/incumbent_warm_dev2/dev2').glob('*_prompts.parquet'):     frame=pd.read_parquet(path)     own=frame.loc[frame.miner_uid.eq(999),['asset','time_length','start_time','crps']].copy()     own=own.rename(columns={'time_length':'length','start_time':'time'})     own['window']='warm_dev2'     own['variant']='incumbent'     own['paths']=1000     rows.append(own) result=pd.concat(rows,ignore_index=True) result.to_parquet('agent/results/warm_incumbent_scores.parquet') print('Extracted warmed incumbent rows:',len(result),flush=True) frames=[pd.read_parquet('agent/results/'+name+'.parquet') for name in ['rank_daily_dense','rank_hourly_dense']] candidate=pd.concat(frames,ignore_index=True) candidate=candidate.loc[candidate.window.eq('dev1')].copy() candidate['variant']='stratified' candidate.to_parquet('agent/results/rank_dev1.parquet') PY OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 uv run agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume > agent/rank_warm_dev2.log 2>&1 & OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 uv run agent/rank_screen.py rank_dev1 --template starter_full --round-step 1 --workers 4 > agent/rank_dev1_rewards.log 2>&1 & wait\n   3340       02:40 uv run agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3341       02:40 uv run agent/rank_screen.py rank_dev1 --template starter_full --round-step 1 --workers 4\n   3346       02:40 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3347       02:40 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/rank_screen.py rank_dev1 --template starter_full --round-step 1 --workers 4\n   3358       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3361       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3362       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3363       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3364       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3365       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3366       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3367       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3368       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3369       02:35 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/research.py --name rank_warm_dev2 --windows warm_dev2 --variants stackrank:increments:1 --variants-high stackrank:prefix:1 --paths 1000 --stride-low 1 --stride-high 1 --workers 10 --resume\n   3398       00:43 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/rank_screen.py rank_dev1 --template starter_full --round-step 1 --workers 4\n   3399       00:43 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/rank_screen.py rank_dev1 --template starter_full --round-step 1 --workers 4\n   3400       00:43 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/rank_screen.py rank_dev1 --template starter_full --round-step 1 --workers 4\n   3401       00:43 /workspace/.venv/bin/python3 agent/rank_screen.py rank_dev1 --template starter_full --round-step 1 --workers 4\n   3409       00:00 /bin/bash -c pwd; cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; head -100 agent/journal.md; git status --short; ps -eo pid,etime,args | rg 'research.py|rank_screen.py|field_eval.py'\n   3415       00:00 rg research.py|rank_screen.py|field_eval.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
