{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_141","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\"'^def |stackphase|stackrank|WINDOWS|variants-high'\"' agent/research.py; cat agent/sampling.py; sed -n '1,180p' agent/report.md; sed -n '1,120p' agent/modeling.py\"","aggregated_output":"25:WINDOWS = {\"dev1\": (\"2026-07-28\", \"2026-08-07\"), \"dev2\": (\"2026-08-08\", \"2026-08-18\"), \"final\": (\"2026-08-18\", \"2026-08-28\"), \"warm_dev2\": (\"2026-07-29\", \"2026-08-18\")}\n28:def seed_for(asset, start_time):\n33:def learned_weights(kind='neural'):\n58:def learned_multiplier(feature, asset, length, kind):\n83:def features(context, start_time, increment):\n145:def generate_variant(feature, asset, start_time, increment, length, count, variant):\n147:    stacked_mode = parts[0] in ['stacked','refresh','refresh_old','stacktick','stackdf','stackperiodic','stackensemble','stacktree','stacklead','stackpersist','stackstudent','stackmix','stacknewton','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase']\n148:    phase_strength = float(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='stackphase' else None\n149:    phase_kind = 'phase_control' if parts[0]=='stackphase' and parts[1]=='control' else 'phase'\n150:    rank_mode = parts[1] if parts[0]=='stackrank' else None\n152:    fine_strength = (1. if asset not in CRYPTO and length>3600 else None) if parts[0] in ['stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stackfine' else None\n155:    temporal_lead_strength = 1. if parts[0] in ['stacknewton','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='stackmix' else float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stacklead' else None\n156:    tree_strength = (1. if length>3600 else None) if parts[0] in ['stackrank','stackphase'] else 1. if parts[0]=='stackfine' else float(parts[1]) if parts[0] in ['stacktree','stackstudent','stackmix'] else None\n159:    tree_kind = 'tree_student' if parts[0] in ['stackstudent','stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else 'tree'\n175:    periodic_strength=(.5 if asset not in CRYPTO and length>3600 else 0.) if parts[0] in ['stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[3]) if parts[0]=='stackmix' else float(parts[1]) if parts[0] in ['periodic','stackperiodic'] else float(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='stacktick' and len(parts)>2 else None\n184:    tick_kind='gcd' if parts[0] in ['stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else parts[1] if parts[0] in ['ticked','stacktick'] else 'min'\n185:    atom_mode='quantize' if parts[0] in ['ticked','stacktick','stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else parts[0] if parts[0] in ['symboot','hurdle','quantize'] else None\n186:    atom_parameter=1. if parts[0] in ['ticked','stacktick','stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[1]) if atom_mode else 0.\n452:def evaluate_asset(task):\n455:    begin, end = [pd.Timestamp(value, tz=\"UTC\") for value in WINDOWS[window]]\n483:def main():\n488:    parser.add_argument(\"--variants-high\")\n\"\"\"Deterministic finite-fan samplers for the same scale-mixture process.\"\"\"\n\nfrom collections import deque\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.special import ndtr, ndtri\nfrom scipy.stats import chi2, qmc, t\n\n\ndef bridge_increments(normals):\n    count, steps = normals.shape\n    paths = np.zeros((count, steps + 1))\n    if steps == 0:\n        return normals.copy()\n    paths[:, steps] = np.sqrt(steps) * normals[:, 0]\n    intervals = deque([(0, steps)])\n    coordinate = 1\n    while intervals:\n        left, right = intervals.popleft()\n        middle = (left + right) // 2\n        if middle == left:\n            continue\n        span = right - left\n        paths[:, middle] = ((right - middle) * paths[:, left] + (middle - left) * paths[:, right]) / span\n        paths[:, middle] += np.sqrt((middle - left) * (right - middle) / span) * normals[:, coordinate]\n        coordinate += 1\n        intervals.extend([(left, middle), (middle, right)])\n    return np.diff(paths, axis=1)\n\n\ndef mixture_shocks(count, steps, seed, mode, degrees=5.):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    pairs = (count + 1) // 2\n    if mode in ['sobol', 'bridge']:\n        engine = qmc.Sobol(steps + 1, scramble=True, seed=int(seed % (2**32)))\n        uniforms = engine.random_base2(int(np.ceil(np.log2(max(1, pairs)))))[:pairs]\n        scales = np.sqrt((degrees - 2) / chi2.ppf(np.clip(uniforms[:, :1], 1e-12, 1 - 1e-12), degrees))\n        normals = ndtri(np.clip(uniforms[:, 1:], 1e-12, 1 - 1e-12))\n        if mode == 'bridge':\n            normals = bridge_increments(normals)\n    else:\n        normals = rng.standard_normal((pairs, steps))\n        if mode == 'paired':\n            scales = np.sqrt(3 / rng.chisquare(5, size=(pairs, 1)))\n        elif mode == 'stratified':\n            quantiles = (np.arange(pairs) + .5) / pairs\n            scales = np.sqrt(3 / chi2.ppf(rng.permutation(quantiles)[:, None], 5))\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(f'Unknown sampler {mode}')\n    shocks = normals * scales\n    return np.concatenate([shocks, -shocks], axis=0)[:count]\n\n\ndef persistent_shocks(count, steps, seed, increment, persistence_minutes):\n    if steps == 0 or np.isinf(persistence_minutes):\n        return mixture_shocks(count, steps, seed, 'sobol')\n    pairs = (count+1)//2\n    power = int(np.ceil(np.log2(max(1, pairs))))\n    engine = qmc.Sobol(steps+1, scramble=True, seed=int(seed % (2**32)))\n    uniforms = engine.random_base2(power)[:pairs]\n    normals = ndtri(np.clip(uniforms[:, 1:], 1e-12, 1-1e-12))\n    block_indices = np.arange(steps)*increment//3600\n    blocks = int(block_indices[-1]+1)\n    scale = np.empty((pairs, blocks))\n    scale[:, 0] = np.sqrt(3/chi2.ppf(np.clip(uniforms[:, 0], 1e-12, 1-1e-12), 5))\n    if blocks > 1:\n        innovations = qmc.Sobol(blocks-1, scramble=True, seed=int((seed ^ 0x6A09E667) % (2**32)))\n        innovations = ndtri(np.clip(innovations.random_base2(power)[:pairs], 1e-12, 1-1e-12))\n        latent = ndtri(np.clip(uniforms[:, 0], 1e-12, 1-1e-12))\n        correlation = np.exp(-60/persistence_minutes)\n        for block in range(1, blocks):\n            latent = correlation*latent+np.sqrt(1-correlation**2)*innovations[:, block-1]\n            scale[:, block] = np.sqrt(3/chi2.ppf(np.clip(ndtr(latent), 1e-12, 1-1e-12), 5))\n    shocks = normals*scale[:, block_indices]\n    return np.concatenate([shocks, -shocks], axis=0)[:count]\n\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=8)\ndef student_quantiles(count, degrees=5.):\n    probabilities = (np.arange(count)+.5)/count\n    return np.sqrt((degrees-2)/degrees)*t.ppf(probabilities, degrees)\n\n\ndef stratified_marginals(values, scales=1.):\n    if values.shape[1] == 0:\n        return values.copy()\n    order = np.argsort(values, axis=0, kind='stable')\n    output = np.empty_like(values)\n    np.put_along_axis(output, order, student_quantiles(len(values))[:, None], axis=0)\n    return output*np.broadcast_to(scales, (values.shape[1],))\n# Better volatility forecasts are not the same as better miner rewards\n\n**Status: interim research report. The campaign is active; no champion has been nominated.**\n\n## Context\n\nSynth SN50 asks a miner for a fan of future prices, not a single price target. This campaign covers all three competitions: five crypto assets at one-minute resolution for one hour, the same assets at five-minute resolution for one day, and eight live commodity/equity assets at five-minute resolution for one day. SPYX is retired and is not modeled or evaluated.\n\nEvery served prompt must contain the requested number of paths—1000 in the validator—and include the observed starting price. The daily and hourly shapes are therefore 1000 by 289 and 1000 by 61. Prices come only from the frozen snapshot, whose research cutoff is August 29, 2026. Nothing from the subsequent evaluation period is used.\n\nThe distinction between CRPS and emissions matters. The validator converts per-prompt CRPS into relative prompt scores, applies asset coefficients, smooths scores, and uses a softmax. An arithmetic average of CRPS across assets is not the campaign objective. The campaign's Score is 100 times the mean of the three capped reward ratios to each competition's best field miner.\n\nIn the pinned code, valid prompt scores are specifically **raw CRPS minus the prompt minimum**. There is no percentile-range division or capping of valid scores; the 95th percentile fills missed responses only. This distinction is important when designing a training loss. Per-prompt division by a realized baseline score is not equivalent to the validator's rule.\n\nThe implementation is anchored to synth-lib commit `782a44fe7585a4e7b0388e22b99c57f13d56f5ee` and its pinned synth-subnet dependency `0d014cbcec376b28e887403b84025ebf1a89f60e`. The source-of-truth functions are `calculate_crps_for_miner`, `compute_prompt_scores`, and `compute_smoothed_score`; scoring and ranking are invoked through `agent/predict.py` and `synth_lib.backtester`, never through a substitute scoring formula.\n\n## Approach\n\n### Start with trustworthy evaluation\n\nThe starter's context loader was strict and rejected legitimate NaNs. It now reads existing parquet partitions directly, preserves missing observations, checks coverage before evaluation, and uses exact archived prompt timestamps. It never repairs missing market prices by interpolation. The current model drops invalid closes and scales returns spanning gaps by the square root of elapsed minutes.\n\nThe offline field bundle begins around July 27, considerably later than the price history. Development therefore uses **scored times July 28–August 7 and August 8–18**, with August 18–28 reserved for final validation. A daily prompt starts one day before its scored time; the model's fitting cutoff precedes the first development prediction, not merely its score date.\n\nThe two development windows genuinely differ in volatility. Average daily realized RMS for BTC is approximately 1.50% versus 0.96%; for ETH, 2.06% versus 1.28%; for SP500, 0.71% versus 0.44%; and for AAPLX, 2.12% versus 0.96%. These are more-volatile and calmer periods, not claims about bull and bear markets.\n\n### The provisional model\n\nThe current incumbent uses a robust exponentially weighted absolute-return scale. Its half-life is one hour for hourly forecasts and six hours for daily forecasts. A constant latent volatility multiplier per path gives a Student-like, five-degree-of-freedom log-return mixture. There is no directional log-drift forecast.\n\nBoth formats additionally use a small pooled neural volatility model, at full strength daily and half strength hourly. Its 30 inputs summarize eight absolute-return and eight RMS scales, their ratios, the absolute volatility level, and asset identity. A 48/32-unit network predicts a multiplicative volatility correction. Training uses 23,457 contexts before July 1, with 2,508 July 1–25 contexts for chronological model selection. All labels end before July 26. Serving uses exported NumPy weights, not a GPU or a network call.\n\nA positive, smoothed daily-clock volatility profile is estimated from the supplied past seven days. Crypto uses 30-minute bins at 0.75 strength; commodities/equities use 15-minute bins at full strength, with shrinkage toward a flat curve. This is **not a market-session model**: there are no opening/closing times, exchange calendars, weekend switches, or zero-volatility hours. The empirical finding is simply that continuous trading does not imply constant variance throughout a day.\n\nA second small network predicts volatility in five future daily buckets: minutes 0–60, 60–180, 180–360, 360–720 and 720–1440. Its output is normalized to unit mean and applied at half strength. Each clock/lead component has unit mean, but their product generally does not: a small diagnostic finds joint means between 0.9992 and 1.0252. Thus the combined model changes both timing and, modestly, average scale. This actual implementation passes full official reward replay in both development windows; explicit joint normalization is a separate ongoing test.\n\nTimestamp-derived random seeds and shared draws make comparisons reproducible. Representative 1000-path serving checks take a few hundredths of a second, preserve arbitrary positive path counts, tolerate NaN-contaminated contexts, and are invariant to injected rows after the prediction time. Learned weights are not applied before their availability date.\n\nBoth formats now additionally use a small temporal convolutional residual over 168 past hourly bins of absolute returns, RMS returns, signed returns and observed coverage. This residual is trained against the frozen static model and exported to NumPy. Direct scrambled Sobol draws replace ordinary draws while preserving the intended five-degree Student scale-mixture family and exactly honoring 1000 paths. Full official reward replay supports both changes together. The implementation is self-contained and does not import Torch at serving time.\n\n## Experiments\n\nDaily scale now additionally uses a numerical tree student distilled from our own pretrained-feature teacher. It uses only the supplied context, existing temporal features and smooth clock terms at serving time; the foundation-model cache is not a runtime dependency. The complete daily combination includes GCD quote projection and a half-strength smooth periodic level correction for commodities/equities. A second, small shape-specific temporal encoder is retained only for COM_EQU after full reward confirmation; the analogous crypto extension is rejected. All deployed learned inference is NumPy/SciPy, without Torch or sklearn imports.\n\nThe promoted daily lead shape now also receives a bounded linear correction from the frozen temporal encoder's 48 hidden features and the static inputs. It preserves the duration-weighted mean of the original bucket forecast before the existing interpolation/normalization. Chronological selection chooses L2 penalty 0.01; every-prompt CRPS and full reward replay confirm the gain in both development windows. Production reuses the temporal embedding instead of evaluating the encoder twice, and the hourly model is unchanged.\n\nThe journal contains preregistered hypotheses, changes, numerical results, and keep/discard decisions. Important findings so far:\n\n| Experiment | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Recent RMS versus absolute-return volatility | Robust recent scale wins consistently; the seven-day starter adapts too slowly. |\n| One-hour versus six-hour memory | Hourly and daily formats need different memories. A short daily memory overreacts. |\n| Gaussian versus Student path mixtures | A modest heavy-tailed mixture improves calibration; extreme dispersion does not. |\n| Five-/15-/30-minute calibration returns | No universal improvement over the minute-based robust estimator. |\n| Hand-designed volatility reversion | Small, inconsistent gains; not retained. |\n| Pooled neural forecasting | Material daily gains, but the full hourly correction does not improve mean CRPS. |\n| IID tails, stochastic volatility, empirical bootstrap | The tested more complicated dynamics do not beat the simple mixture consistently. |\n| Official-score-curve fine-tuning | 95,750 official training scores produce only a tiny interpolation-loss gain that does not transfer consistently. |\n| Positive local periodic profile | Improves both daily competitions across both development regimes. |\n| Moment-matched tail grids | No consistent material improvement over the incumbent mixture. |\n| Historical profile prior and alternative short memories | Added complexity has negligible or mixed gains. |\n| Recency-weighted neural refitting | Small, consistent CRPS gains; retained for reward-based confirmation, not automatically adopted. |\n| Serial return correlation | Estimated correlation is essentially a null result; fixed correlations overfit regimes. |\n| Direct high/low range substitution | Does not transfer consistently; a fixed diffusion-based range calibration is inadequate. |\n| Frozen temporal lead correction | Improves both daily competitions in full official reward replay; promoted. |\n| Time-varying Student scale with unchanged one-step marginals | Tiny dev1 gains reverse in dev2; reject finite scale persistence. |\n| Pretrained-feature shallow trees | Improve daily CRPS but hurt hourly; daily full reward confirmation is pending. |\n| Numerical teacher distillation | A 1.7 MiB student approximates the 183 MiB pretrained-feature pipeline and preserves initial daily CRPS gains; dense interaction confirmation is pending. |\n\nDense hourly reward replays confirm that small mean-CRPS differences can produce large changes in emissions. The auxiliary OHLCV network and most empirical/discrete-return variants do not pass the consistency gate. Correcting the score-curve objective to raw, asset-weighted CRPS also fails to produce a consistent fitted gain; it remains a methodological correction, not a successful model improvement. Five-minute supervised targets, conditional directional ridge forecasts, forecast-error scale mixtures and context-only moment recalibration are rejected. A finer score-response grid resolves a coarse-surrogate optimization issue, but its development gains still do not transfer consistently, including the bounded 600-epoch extension. The exact-context temporal residual and Sobol sampler are promoted on both formats; finer daily clock profiles also pass full reward confirmation.\n\nAn implementation failure is included rather than hidden: the first training-label build treated microsecond timestamps as nanoseconds, inflating scale ratios by the square root of 1000. Target-distribution checks caught it before any learned candidate was scored. Those artifacts were discarded, timestamp units were made explicit, and training was rebuilt.\n\n## Results\n\nFull 1000-path starter replays produce Score estimates **0.32375** and **0.42456** on the two development windows.\n\nFull official reward replay supports the current temporal/Sobol incumbent:\n\n| Window | Competition | Starter reward rank | Incumbent reward rank | Incumbent reward / best field reward |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| dev1 | COM_EQU_24H | 239 / 257 | 111 / 257 | 0.056455 |\n| dev1 | CRYPTO_1H | 216 / 257 | 25 / 257 | 0.950966 |\n| dev1 | CRYPTO_24H | 236 / 257 | 33 / 257 | 0.395023 |\n| dev2 | COM_EQU_24H | 248 / 257 | 200 / 257 | 0.033241 |\n| dev2 | CRYPTO_1H | 227 / 257 | 9 / 257 | 0.966341 |\n| dev2 | CRYPTO_24H | 257 / 257 | 86 / 257 | 0.241880 |\n\nThese correspond to development selection Score proxies **46.748** and **41.382**, versus 0.32375 and 0.42456 for the starter under the same short-window protocol. They are not clean steady-state estimates: the official warmup trimmer is a no-op when trimming would remove all rounds, which affects these ten-day daily runs. An explicit ten-day simulation warmup followed by scoring only dev2 is in progress. The improvement is substantial, but not cumulative field leadership. Hourly final-round ranks are 55 and 1, distinct from cumulative ranks 25 and 9. Each row uses all available official reward rounds for its selected candidate; hourly has 1,624/1,847 rounds, daily crypto 1,015/1,044, and COM_EQU 1,499/1,474. Production and research paths match to relative tolerance 1e-12 on representative assets and both formats. The reserved final validation remains untouched.\n\nThese are historical reward estimates, not the campaign's eventual post-cutoff Score. Sampled terminal-rank diagnostics and reward-round-thinned screens are explicitly labeled and are not substituted for full final replay.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The effective validation sample consists of market days and regimes, not thousands of independent prompt observations. Overlapping horizons create dependence.\n- Many hypotheses are screened on the same development windows. The reserved final window remains necessary, and small grid gains may be selection noise.\n- Internal likelihood diagnostics split rows by prediction timestamp; adjacent 24-hour targets can overlap at that split boundary. They are model-selection diagnostics, not causal field backtests. Every field-scored forecast starts after the entire fitting-label cutoff.\n- Response curves around already fitted parameters are meta-training objectives, not claims of causal historical forecasting performance. All reported candidate CRPS and rewards are evaluated later through the official scorer.\n- The pinned configuration uses ten-day daily and five-day hourly smoothing windows. Short runs can bypass warmup trimming altogether. Reports must state the actual intervals and distinguish comparative development selection from explicitly warmed steady-state estimates, rather than repeat the campaign prose's 24-hour description.\n- The official backtester may replace NaN-holed realized paths with cached validator truth. This behavior is preserved, not silently altered.\n- A context with no finite positive close cannot supply the required starting price; the model raises a clear error instead of inventing one.\n- The frozen-data interface does not provide contemporaneous cross-asset information or external news. Additional information used by live miners may not be reproducible here.\n- A bounded historical score-only API probe returned out-of-request timestamps and was rejected before any score analysis or storage. Evaluation remains strictly offline, with a fail-closed environment check.\n\n## What we would do with more budget\n\nThe campaign still has budget and is continuing. Priorities are to confirm temporal/lead interactions, finish finite-ensemble and hourly emission-sensitive selection, and stress-test chronological calibration. Any additional fitted checkpoint must be available before the predictions it evaluates. Final nomination requires a frozen model, a full 1000-path field replay with explicit warmup handling, interface checks, and committed artifacts—not an attractive development-only CRPS table.\n\"\"\"Offline probabilistic paths with robust, horizon-specific volatility forecasts.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom scipy.special import ndtri\nfrom scipy.stats import chi2, qmc\n\nASSETS = (\"BTC\", \"ETH\", \"SOL\", \"XRP\", \"HYPE\", \"XAU\", \"NVDAX\", \"GOOGLX\", \"TSLAX\", \"AAPLX\", \"WTIOIL\", \"SPCX\", \"SP500\")\nHALF_LIVES = (30, 60, 180, 360, 720, 1440, 2880, 10080)\nCOMMODITY_EQUITY_BLEND = 1.0\nCYCLE_STRENGTH = 0.75\nLEAD_STRENGTH = 0.5\nWEIGHTS_AVAILABLE_FROM = pd.Timestamp('2026-07-26',tz='UTC')\n\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=8)\ndef _weights(kind='volatility'):\n    with np.load(Path(__file__).with_name(f\"{kind}_weights.npz\"), allow_pickle=False) as archive:\n        return {name: archive[name] for name in archive.files}\n\n\ndef _features(closes, timestamp):\n    minutes = (closes.index - timestamp).total_seconds().to_numpy() / 60\n    gaps = np.diff(minutes)\n    returns = np.diff(np.log(closes.to_numpy(dtype=float)))\n    valid = (gaps > 0) & np.isfinite(returns)\n    normalized = returns[valid] / np.sqrt(gaps[valid])\n    age = -minutes[1:][valid]\n    absolute, root_square = {}, {}\n    for half_life in HALF_LIVES:\n        if not normalized.size:\n            absolute[half_life] = root_square[half_life] = 1e-8\n            continue\n        weights = np.exp(-np.log(2) * age / half_life)\n        absolute[half_life] = max(float(np.average(np.abs(normalized), weights=weights) * np.sqrt(np.pi / 2)), 1e-8)\n        root_square[half_life] = max(float(np.sqrt(np.average(normalized ** 2, weights=weights))), 1e-8)\n    return absolute, root_square\n\n\ndef _feature_values(absolute, root_square, asset):\n    reference = absolute[360]\n    values = [np.log(max(estimator[half_life], 1e-9) / reference)\n              for estimator in (absolute, root_square) for half_life in HALF_LIVES]\n    values += [np.log(reference), *[float(asset == name) for name in ASSETS]]\n    return np.asarray(values,dtype=np.float32)\n\n\ndef _network_output(values, weights):\n    inputs = np.clip((values - weights[\"center\"]) / weights[\"spread\"], -8, 8)\n    for layer in (0, 2):\n        inputs = weights[f\"{layer}.weight\"] @ inputs + weights[f\"{layer}.bias\"]\n        inputs = inputs / (1 + np.exp(-inputs))\n    return weights[\"4.weight\"] @ inputs + weights[\"4.bias\"]\n\n\ndef _learned_multiplier(absolute, root_square, asset, hourly):\n    output = _network_output(_feature_values(absolute,root_square,asset),_weights())\n    strength = .5 if hourly else 1.0 if asset in ASSETS[:5] else COMMODITY_EQUITY_BLEND\n    return float(np.exp(np.clip(output[0 if hourly else 1], -2, 2))) ** strength\n\n\ndef _lead_profile(absolute, root_square, asset, steps, increment, embedding, sequence):\n    if steps == 0:\n        return np.ones(0)\n    weights = _weights('lead')\n    buckets = np.exp(np.clip(_network_output(_feature_values(absolute,root_square,asset),weights),-2,2))\n    correction_weights = _weights('lead_temporal')\n    standardized = np.clip((_feature_values(absolute,root_square,asset)-correction_weights['static_center'])/correction_weights['static_spread'],-8,8)\n    design = np.r_[embedding,standardized]\n    design = np.r_[1.,np.clip((design-correction_weights['center'])/correction_weights['spread'],-8,8)]\n    adjusted = buckets*np.exp(np.clip(design @ correction_weights['coefficients'],-.5,.5))\n    duration = correction_weights['duration']\n    buckets = adjusted*(buckets @ duration)/(adjusted @ duration)\n    if asset not in ASSETS[:5]:\n        fine = _weights('lead_finetune')\n        hidden = _sequence_embedding(sequence,_feature_values(absolute,root_square,asset),fine)\n        correction = np.clip(fine['head.2.weight'] @ hidden+fine['head.2.bias'],-.5,.5)\n        adjusted = buckets*np.exp(correction)\n        duration = fine['duration']\n        buckets = adjusted*(buckets @ duration)/(adjusted @ duration)\n    centers = (weights['edges'][:-1]+weights['edges'][1:])/2\n    shape = np.interp(np.arange(1,steps+1)*increment/60,centers,buckets)\n    shape = np.clip(shape/shape.mean(),.4,2.5)**LEAD_STRENGTH\n    return shape/shape.mean()\n\n\ndef _temporal_embedding(closes, timestamp, absolute, root_square, asset):\n    weights = _weights('temporal_exact')\n    reference = absolute[360]\n    gaps = np.diff(closes.index.as_unit('ns').asi8)/60e9\n    changes = np.diff(np.log(closes.to_numpy()))/np.sqrt(gaps)\n    observed = pd.DataFrame({'absolute':np.abs(changes),'square':changes**2,'signed':changes},index=closes.index[1:])\n    grouped = observed.resample('1h',origin=timestamp.floor('min'),closed='right',label='right')\n    rows = grouped.mean()\n    rows['coverage'] = grouped['absolute'].count()/60\n    rows = rows.reindex(pd.date_range(end=timestamp.floor('min'),periods=168,freq='1h'))\n    values = np.stack([np.log(np.maximum(rows.absolute.to_numpy()*np.sqrt(np.pi/2),1e-9)/reference),\n                       np.log(np.maximum(np.sqrt(rows.square.to_numpy()),1e-9)/reference),\n                       rows.signed.to_numpy()/reference,rows.coverage.to_numpy()])\n    values = np.clip(np.nan_to_num(values,nan=0.,posinf=5.,neginf=-5.),-5,5).astype(np.float32)\n    return _sequence_embedding(values,_feature_values(absolute,root_square,asset),weights), values\n\n\ndef _sequence_embedding(sequence, static, weights):\n    values = sequence\n    for layer in [0,2,4]:\n        padded = np.pad(values,((0,0),(2,2)))\n        windows = np.lib.stride_tricks.sliding_window_view(padded,5,axis=1)[:,::2]\n        values = np.einsum('ock,ctk->ot',weights[f'encoder.{layer}.weight'],windows)+weights[f'encoder.{layer}.bias'][:,None]\n        values = values/(1+np.exp(-values))\n    static = np.clip((static-weights['center'])/weights['spread'],-8,8)\n    values = np.r_[values.reshape(-1),static]\n    values = weights['head.0.weight'] @ values+weights['head.0.bias']\n    values = values/(1+np.exp(-values))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_142","type":"agent_message","text":"The warmed baseline is complete; stratification confirmation is still running.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_143","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '145,210p' agent/research.py; sed -n '483,560p' agent/research.py; cat agent/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_143","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '145,210p' agent/research.py; sed -n '483,560p' agent/research.py; cat agent/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"def generate_variant(feature, asset, start_time, increment, length, count, variant):\n    parts = variant.split(\":\")\n    stacked_mode = parts[0] in ['stacked','refresh','refresh_old','stacktick','stackdf','stackperiodic','stackensemble','stacktree','stacklead','stackpersist','stackstudent','stackmix','stacknewton','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase']\n    phase_strength = float(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='stackphase' else None\n    phase_kind = 'phase_control' if parts[0]=='stackphase' and parts[1]=='control' else 'phase'\n    rank_mode = parts[1] if parts[0]=='stackrank' else None\n    rank_strength = float(parts[2]) if rank_mode else 0.\n    fine_strength = (1. if asset not in CRYPTO and length>3600 else None) if parts[0] in ['stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stackfine' else None\n    newton_strength = float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stacknewton' else None\n    sampler_persistence = float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stackpersist' else None\n    temporal_lead_strength = 1. if parts[0] in ['stacknewton','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='stackmix' else float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stacklead' else None\n    tree_strength = (1. if length>3600 else None) if parts[0] in ['stackrank','stackphase'] else 1. if parts[0]=='stackfine' else float(parts[1]) if parts[0] in ['stacktree','stackstudent','stackmix'] else None\n    if tree_strength == 0:\n        tree_strength = None\n    tree_kind = 'tree_student' if parts[0] in ['stackstudent','stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else 'tree'\n    temporal_ensemble_strength = float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stackensemble' else None\n    sampler_degrees = float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='stackdf' else 5.\n    refresh_strength = float(parts[1]) if parts[0] in ['refresh','refresh_old'] else None\n    refresh_kind = 'refit_recent60' if parts[0]=='refresh_old' else 'refresh'\n    offset = int(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='resample' else int(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='sampler' and len(parts)>2 else 0\n    draw_seed = (seed_for(asset,start_time)+offset) % (2**64)\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(draw_seed)\n    steps = length // increment\n    lead_strength=.5 if stacked_mode or parts[0] in ['location','periodic','uncertainty','clock','sharp','adaptive','calibration','calibration600','ticked'] else float(parts[2]) if parts[0] in ['combined','combined_exact'] else float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='lead' else None\n    sampler='sobol' if stacked_mode or parts[0]=='ticked' else parts[5] if parts[0]=='sharp' and len(parts)>5 else parts[3] if parts[0] in ['combined','combined_exact'] and len(parts)>3 else parts[1] if parts[0]=='sampler' else None\n    fiveminute_strength=float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='fiveminute' else None\n    temporal_strength=1. if stacked_mode or parts[0]=='ticked' else float(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='sharp' else float(parts[1]) if parts[0] in ['temporal','temporal_exact','combined','combined_exact'] else None\n    temporal_kind='temporal_exact' if stacked_mode or parts[0] in ['temporal_exact','combined_exact','sharp','ticked'] else 'temporal'\n    ensemble_strength=float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='ensemble' else None\n    location_strength=float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='location' else None\n    periodic_strength=(.5 if asset not in CRYPTO and length>3600 else 0.) if parts[0] in ['stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[3]) if parts[0]=='stackmix' else float(parts[1]) if parts[0] in ['periodic','stackperiodic'] else float(parts[2]) if parts[0]=='stacktick' and len(parts)>2 else None\n    uncertainty_parameters=parts[1:] if parts[0]=='uncertainty' else None\n    clock_parameters=(['48','.75','.25'] if asset in CRYPTO else ['96','1','.25']) if stacked_mode else [parts[1],parts[3],'0.25'] if parts[0]=='sharp' else parts[1:] if parts[0]=='clock' else None\n    joint_normalize=bool(int(parts[4])) if parts[0]=='sharp' else False\n    adaptive_strength=float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='adaptive' else None\n    calibration_strength=float(parts[1]) if parts[0] in ['calibration','calibration600'] else None\n    calibration_kind=parts[0] if calibration_strength is not None else 'calibration'\n    weighted_strength=float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='weighted' else None\n    tuned_mode=parts[0] in ['tuned','resample'] or lead_strength is not None or sampler is not None or fiveminute_strength is not None or temporal_strength is not None or ensemble_strength is not None\n    tick_kind='gcd' if parts[0] in ['stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else parts[1] if parts[0] in ['ticked','stacktick'] else 'min'\n    atom_mode='quantize' if parts[0] in ['ticked','stacktick','stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else parts[0] if parts[0] in ['symboot','hurdle','quantize'] else None\n    atom_parameter=1. if parts[0] in ['ticked','stacktick','stackmix','stackfine','stackrank','stackphase'] else float(parts[1]) if atom_mode else 0.\n    auxiliary_strength=float(parts[1]) if parts[0]=='aux' else None\n    range_parameters = parts[1:] if parts[0] == 'range' else None\n    correlation_parameter = parts[1] if parts[0] == 'corr' else None\n    prior_strength = float(parts[1]) if parts[0] == 'prior' else 0.\n    refit_parameters = parts[1:] if parts[0] == 'refit' else None\n    if parts[0] == 'quick':\n        parts = ['av',parts[1],'1','5']\n    shape_parameters = parts[1:] if parts[0] == \"shape\" else None\n    cycle_strength = float(parts[1]) if parts[0] == \"cycle\" else 0.\n    if prior_strength:\n        cycle_strength = .75\n        parts = ['hybrid','1']\n    if refit_parameters:\n        cycle_strength = .75\n        parts = ['auto','0','1','5'] if length == 3600 else ['ml','refit_'+refit_parameters[0],refit_parameters[1]]\n    if correlation_parameter is not None:\n        cycle_strength = .75\n        parts = ['hybrid','1']\n    if range_parameters:\n        cycle_strength = .75\n        parts = ['hybrid','1']\n    if auxiliary_strength is not None:\n        cycle_strength=.75\n        parts=['auto','0','1','5']\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument(\"--name\", required=True)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--variants\", default=\"starter,rv:360,rv:1440,av:360,av:1440\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--variants-equity\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--variants-high\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--windows\", default=\"dev1,dev2\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--assets\", default=\",\".join(CRYPTO + EQUITY))\n    parser.add_argument(\"--paths\", type=int, default=256)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--stride-low\", type=int, default=4)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--stride-high\", type=int, default=24)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--workers\", type=int, default=10)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--resume\", action=\"store_true\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--format\", choices=['all','low','high'],default='all')\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    print((AGENT_DIR / \"BUDGET.md\").read_text(), flush=True)\n    variants = args.variants.split(\",\")\n    tasks = [(asset, length, window, args.variants_high.split(',') if length==3600 and args.variants_high else args.variants_equity.split(',') if asset in EQUITY and args.variants_equity else variants,\n              args.paths, args.stride_high if length == 3600 else args.stride_low)\n             for window in args.windows.split(\",\") for asset in args.assets.split(\",\")\n             for length in ([86400, 3600] if asset in CRYPTO else [86400])\n             if args.format=='all' or (args.format=='low' and length==86400) or (args.format=='high' and length==3600)]\n    target = AGENT_DIR / \"results\" / f\"{args.name}.parquet\"\n    target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    checkpoints = target.with_suffix('.parts')\n    checkpoints.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    fingerprint = hashlib.sha256()\n    for source in sorted([*AGENT_DIR.glob('*.py'), *AGENT_DIR.glob('*_weights.npz')]):\n        fingerprint.update(source.name.encode())\n        fingerprint.update(source.read_bytes())\n    results = []\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=args.workers) as executor:\n        pending = {}\n        for task in tasks:\n            identity = json.dumps([task, fingerprint.hexdigest()]).encode()\n            checkpoint = checkpoints / f'{hashlib.sha256(identity).hexdigest()}.parquet'\n            if args.resume and checkpoint.exists():\n                results.append(pd.read_parquet(checkpoint))\n                print(f'Resumed {task[:3]}', flush=True)\n            else:\n                pending[executor.submit(evaluate_asset, task)] = checkpoint\n        for completed in as_completed(pending):\n            batch = pd.DataFrame(completed.result())\n            checkpoint = pending[completed]\n            temporary = checkpoint.with_suffix('.tmp')\n            batch.to_parquet(temporary)\n            temporary.replace(checkpoint)\n            results.append(batch)\n    result = pd.concat(results, ignore_index=True).sort_values(['window', 'asset', 'length', 'time', 'variant'])\n    result.to_parquet(target)\n    print(result.groupby([\"window\", \"length\", \"asset\", \"variant\"]).crps.mean().to_string(), flush=True)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n# Better volatility forecasts are not the same as better miner rewards\n\n**Status: interim research report. The campaign is active; no champion has been nominated.**\n\n## Context\n\nSynth SN50 asks a miner for a fan of future prices, not a single price target. This campaign covers all three competitions: five crypto assets at one-minute resolution for one hour, the same assets at five-minute resolution for one day, and eight live commodity/equity assets at five-minute resolution for one day. SPYX is retired and is not modeled or evaluated.\n\nEvery served prompt must contain the requested number of paths—1000 in the validator—and include the observed starting price. The daily and hourly shapes are therefore 1000 by 289 and 1000 by 61. Prices come only from the frozen snapshot, whose research cutoff is August 29, 2026. Nothing from the subsequent evaluation period is used.\n\nThe distinction between CRPS and emissions matters. The validator converts per-prompt CRPS into relative prompt scores, applies asset coefficients, smooths scores, and uses a softmax. An arithmetic average of CRPS across assets is not the campaign objective. The campaign's Score is 100 times the mean of the three capped reward ratios to each competition's best field miner.\n\nIn the pinned code, valid prompt scores are specifically **raw CRPS minus the prompt minimum**. There is no percentile-range division or capping of valid scores; the 95th percentile fills missed responses only. This distinction is important when designing a training loss. Per-prompt division by a realized baseline score is not equivalent to the validator's rule.\n\nThe implementation is anchored to synth-lib commit `782a44fe7585a4e7b0388e22b99c57f13d56f5ee` and its pinned synth-subnet dependency `0d014cbcec376b28e887403b84025ebf1a89f60e`. The source-of-truth functions are `calculate_crps_for_miner`, `compute_prompt_scores`, and `compute_smoothed_score`; scoring and ranking are invoked through `agent/predict.py` and `synth_lib.backtester`, never through a substitute scoring formula.\n\n## Approach\n\n### Start with trustworthy evaluation\n\nThe starter's context loader was strict and rejected legitimate NaNs. It now reads existing parquet partitions directly, preserves missing observations, checks coverage before evaluation, and uses exact archived prompt timestamps. It never repairs missing market prices by interpolation. The current model drops invalid closes and scales returns spanning gaps by the square root of elapsed minutes.\n\nThe offline field bundle begins around July 27, considerably later than the price history. Development therefore uses **scored times July 28–August 7 and August 8–18**, with August 18–28 reserved for final validation. A daily prompt starts one day before its scored time; the model's fitting cutoff precedes the first development prediction, not merely its score date.\n\nThe two development windows genuinely differ in volatility. Average daily realized RMS for BTC is approximately 1.50% versus 0.96%; for ETH, 2.06% versus 1.28%; for SP500, 0.71% versus 0.44%; and for AAPLX, 2.12% versus 0.96%. These are more-volatile and calmer periods, not claims about bull and bear markets.\n\n### The provisional model\n\nThe current incumbent uses a robust exponentially weighted absolute-return scale. Its half-life is one hour for hourly forecasts and six hours for daily forecasts. A constant latent volatility multiplier per path gives a Student-like, five-degree-of-freedom log-return mixture. There is no directional log-drift forecast.\n\nBoth formats additionally use a small pooled neural volatility model, at full strength daily and half strength hourly. Its 30 inputs summarize eight absolute-return and eight RMS scales, their ratios, the absolute volatility level, and asset identity. A 48/32-unit network predicts a multiplicative volatility correction. Training uses 23,457 contexts before July 1, with 2,508 July 1–25 contexts for chronological model selection. All labels end before July 26. Serving uses exported NumPy weights, not a GPU or a network call.\n\nA positive, smoothed daily-clock volatility profile is estimated from the supplied past seven days. Crypto uses 30-minute bins at 0.75 strength; commodities/equities use 15-minute bins at full strength, with shrinkage toward a flat curve. This is **not a market-session model**: there are no opening/closing times, exchange calendars, weekend switches, or zero-volatility hours. The empirical finding is simply that continuous trading does not imply constant variance throughout a day.\n\nA second small network predicts volatility in five future daily buckets: minutes 0–60, 60–180, 180–360, 360–720 and 720–1440. Its output is normalized to unit mean and applied at half strength. Each clock/lead component has unit mean, but their product generally does not: a small diagnostic finds joint means between 0.9992 and 1.0252. Thus the combined model changes both timing and, modestly, average scale. This actual implementation passes full official reward replay in both development windows; explicit joint normalization is a separate ongoing test.\n\nTimestamp-derived random seeds and shared draws make comparisons reproducible. Representative 1000-path serving checks take a few hundredths of a second, preserve arbitrary positive path counts, tolerate NaN-contaminated contexts, and are invariant to injected rows after the prediction time. Learned weights are not applied before their availability date.\n\nBoth formats now additionally use a small temporal convolutional residual over 168 past hourly bins of absolute returns, RMS returns, signed returns and observed coverage. This residual is trained against the frozen static model and exported to NumPy. Direct scrambled Sobol draws replace ordinary draws while preserving the intended five-degree Student scale-mixture family and exactly honoring 1000 paths. Full official reward replay supports both changes together. The implementation is self-contained and does not import Torch at serving time.\n\n## Experiments\n\nDaily scale now additionally uses a numerical tree student distilled from our own pretrained-feature teacher. It uses only the supplied context, existing temporal features and smooth clock terms at serving time; the foundation-model cache is not a runtime dependency. The complete daily combination includes GCD quote projection and a half-strength smooth periodic level correction for commodities/equities. A second, small shape-specific temporal encoder is retained only for COM_EQU after full reward confirmation; the analogous crypto extension is rejected. All deployed learned inference is NumPy/SciPy, without Torch or sklearn imports.\n\nThe promoted daily lead shape now also receives a bounded linear correction from the frozen temporal encoder's 48 hidden features and the static inputs. It preserves the duration-weighted mean of the original bucket forecast before the existing interpolation/normalization. Chronological selection chooses L2 penalty 0.01; every-prompt CRPS and full reward replay confirm the gain in both development windows. Production reuses the temporal embedding instead of evaluating the encoder twice, and the hourly model is unchanged.\n\nThe journal contains preregistered hypotheses, changes, numerical results, and keep/discard decisions. Important findings so far:\n\n| Experiment | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Recent RMS versus absolute-return volatility | Robust recent scale wins consistently; the seven-day starter adapts too slowly. |\n| One-hour versus six-hour memory | Hourly and daily formats need different memories. A short daily memory overreacts. |\n| Gaussian versus Student path mixtures | A modest heavy-tailed mixture improves calibration; extreme dispersion does not. |\n| Five-/15-/30-minute calibration returns | No universal improvement over the minute-based robust estimator. |\n| Hand-designed volatility reversion | Small, inconsistent gains; not retained. |\n| Pooled neural forecasting | Material daily gains, but the full hourly correction does not improve mean CRPS. |\n| IID tails, stochastic volatility, empirical bootstrap | The tested more complicated dynamics do not beat the simple mixture consistently. |\n| Official-score-curve fine-tuning | 95,750 official training scores produce only a tiny interpolation-loss gain that does not transfer consistently. |\n| Positive local periodic profile | Improves both daily competitions across both development regimes. |\n| Moment-matched tail grids | No consistent material improvement over the incumbent mixture. |\n| Historical profile prior and alternative short memories | Added complexity has negligible or mixed gains. |\n| Recency-weighted neural refitting | Small, consistent CRPS gains; retained for reward-based confirmation, not automatically adopted. |\n| Serial return correlation | Estimated correlation is essentially a null result; fixed correlations overfit regimes. |\n| Direct high/low range substitution | Does not transfer consistently; a fixed diffusion-based range calibration is inadequate. |\n| Frozen temporal lead correction | Improves both daily competitions in full official reward replay; promoted. |\n| Time-varying Student scale with unchanged one-step marginals | Tiny dev1 gains reverse in dev2; reject finite scale persistence. |\n| Pretrained-feature shallow trees | Improve daily CRPS but hurt hourly; daily full reward confirmation is pending. |\n| Numerical teacher distillation | A 1.7 MiB student approximates the 183 MiB pretrained-feature pipeline and preserves initial daily CRPS gains; dense interaction confirmation is pending. |\n\nDense hourly reward replays confirm that small mean-CRPS differences can produce large changes in emissions. The auxiliary OHLCV network and most empirical/discrete-return variants do not pass the consistency gate. Correcting the score-curve objective to raw, asset-weighted CRPS also fails to produce a consistent fitted gain; it remains a methodological correction, not a successful model improvement. Five-minute supervised targets, conditional directional ridge forecasts, forecast-error scale mixtures and context-only moment recalibration are rejected. A finer score-response grid resolves a coarse-surrogate optimization issue, but its development gains still do not transfer consistently, including the bounded 600-epoch extension. The exact-context temporal residual and Sobol sampler are promoted on both formats; finer daily clock profiles also pass full reward confirmation.\n\nAn implementation failure is included rather than hidden: the first training-label build treated microsecond timestamps as nanoseconds, inflating scale ratios by the square root of 1000. Target-distribution checks caught it before any learned candidate was scored. Those artifacts were discarded, timestamp units were made explicit, and training was rebuilt.\n\n## Results\n\nFull 1000-path starter replays produce Score estimates **0.32375** and **0.42456** on the two development windows.\n\nFull official reward replay supports the current temporal/Sobol incumbent:\n\n| Window | Competition | Starter reward rank | Incumbent reward rank | Incumbent reward / best field reward |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| dev1 | COM_EQU_24H | 239 / 257 | 111 / 257 | 0.056455 |\n| dev1 | CRYPTO_1H | 216 / 257 | 25 / 257 | 0.950966 |\n| dev1 | CRYPTO_24H | 236 / 257 | 33 / 257 | 0.395023 |\n| dev2 | COM_EQU_24H | 248 / 257 | 200 / 257 | 0.033241 |\n| dev2 | CRYPTO_1H | 227 / 257 | 9 / 257 | 0.966341 |\n| dev2 | CRYPTO_24H | 257 / 257 | 86 / 257 | 0.241880 |\n\nThese correspond to development selection Score proxies **46.748** and **41.382**, versus 0.32375 and 0.42456 for the starter under the same short-window protocol. They are not clean steady-state estimates: the official warmup trimmer is a no-op when trimming would remove all rounds, which affects these ten-day daily runs. An explicit ten-day simulation warmup followed by scoring only dev2 is in progress. The improvement is substantial, but not cumulative field leadership. Hourly final-round ranks are 55 and 1, distinct from cumulative ranks 25 and 9. Each row uses all available official reward rounds for its selected candidate; hourly has 1,624/1,847 rounds, daily crypto 1,015/1,044, and COM_EQU 1,499/1,474. Production and research paths match to relative tolerance 1e-12 on representative assets and both formats. The reserved final validation remains untouched.\n\nThese are historical reward estimates, not the campaign's eventual post-cutoff Score. Sampled terminal-rank diagnostics and reward-round-thinned screens are explicitly labeled and are not substituted for full final replay.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The effective validation sample consists of market days and regimes, not thousands of independent prompt observations. Overlapping horizons create dependence.\n- Many hypotheses are screened on the same development windows. The reserved final window remains necessary, and small grid gains may be selection noise.\n- Internal likelihood diagnostics split rows by prediction timestamp; adjacent 24-hour targets can overlap at that split boundary. They are model-selection diagnostics, not causal field backtests. Every field-scored forecast starts after the entire fitting-label cutoff.\n- Response curves around already fitted parameters are meta-training objectives, not claims of causal historical forecasting performance. All reported candidate CRPS and rewards are evaluated later through the official scorer.\n- The pinned configuration uses ten-day daily and five-day hourly smoothing windows. Short runs can bypass warmup trimming altogether. Reports must state the actual intervals and distinguish comparative development selection from explicitly warmed steady-state estimates, rather than repeat the campaign prose's 24-hour description.\n- The official backtester may replace NaN-holed realized paths with cached validator truth. This behavior is preserved, not silently altered.\n- A context with no finite positive close cannot supply the required starting price; the model raises a clear error instead of inventing one.\n- The frozen-data interface does not provide contemporaneous cross-asset information or external news. Additional information used by live miners may not be reproducible here.\n- A bounded historical score-only API probe returned out-of-request timestamps and was rejected before any score analysis or storage. Evaluation remains strictly offline, with a fail-closed environment check.\n\n## What we would do with more budget\n\nThe campaign still has budget and is continuing. Priorities are to confirm temporal/lead interactions, finish finite-ensemble and hourly emission-sensitive selection, and stress-test chronological calibration. Any additional fitted checkpoint must be available before the predictions it evaluates. Final nomination requires a frozen model, a full 1000-path field replay with explicit warmup handling, interface checks, and committed artifacts—not an attractive development-only CRPS table.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
