{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_174","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: /workspace/agent/report.md\n@@\n An implementation failure is included rather than hidden: the first training-label build treated microsecond timestamps as nanoseconds, inflating scale ratios by the square root of 1000. Target-distribution checks caught it before any learned candidate was scored. Those artifacts were discarded, timestamp units were made explicit, and training was rebuilt.\n+\n+### Why finite-fan construction matters\n+\n+For a fixed continuous predictive distribution, an equally weighted finite ensemble at midpoint quantiles minimizes expected empirical-ensemble CRPS. This is a property of the submitted finite distribution, not evidence that its underlying forecast is correct. Zamo and Naveau (2018), *Estimation of the Continuous Ranked Probability Score with Limited Information and Applications to Ensemble Weather Forecasts*, Mathematical Geosciences, DOI \"'`10.1007/s11004-017-9709-7`, discuss this distinction in Section 3.2.1 and attribute the optimal quantile result to Bröcker (2012).\n+\n+Our sampler retains each simulated coordinate'\"'s rank while replacing its values with the intended Student quantiles. The difficulty is joint consistency: correcting price-level marginals can disturb increment marginals, and conversely. Consequently the univariate result does not prove improvement for Synth's multi-interval objective. Daily prefix remapping actually fails our development consistency check. Daily increment remapping and hourly prefix remapping pass dense CRPS checks, and a fixed multiscale daily projection receives its own separate preregistration. We never replace the official scorer with a favorable alternative estimator.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\napply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: /workspace/agent/journal.md\n@@\n # Research journal — campaign-3\n+\n+### Iteration 47 first full reward confirmation\n+\n+Dev1 all-round reward replay confirms stratification in all three competitions: COM_EQU ratio 0.0598841, rank 111/257; hourly crypto 0.9719845, rank 21/257; daily crypto 0.4206283, rank 32/257. The corresponding short-window Score proxy is 48.41656, versus approximately 46.748 for the deployed model. This is still a comparative short-window result, not a steady-state estimate. The warmed dev2 incumbent replay has exactly reproduced its COM_EQU golden ratio and rank; remaining competitions and the stratified candidate are running.\n+\n+The knowledge-only literature check finds the same midpoint-quantile rationale in Zamo and Naveau (2018), DOI \"'`10.1007/s11004-017-9709-7`, Section 3.2.1. It does not validate our joint path transformation; only the official historical comparisons can do that. No external prices or post-cutoff field data were requested.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\ngit add agent/fit_phase.py agent/journal.md agent/rank_screen.py agent/report.md agent/research.py agent/sampling.py agent/phase_control_weights.npz agent/phase_weights.json agent/phase_weights.npz agent/results/incumbent_contract_audit.json agent/results/incumbent_warm_dev2 agent/results/phase_screen.parquet agent/results/rank_daily_dense.parquet agent/results/rank_dev1.parquet agent/results/rank_dev1_template_step1_ranks.json agent/results/rank_hourly_dense.parquet agent/results/rank_stratification_screen.parquet agent/results/rank_warm_compare.parquet agent/results/rank_warm_dev2.parquet agent/results/warm_incumbent_scores.parquet\ngit commit -m '\"'Confirm finite-fan stratification and document exact warmed scoring baseline'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
